WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:09.314
A transportation mode is a method for moving people or goods, supported by infrastructure, vehicles, operators, rules, and energy.

2
00:00:09.314 --> 00:00:18.751
The source chapter ranges from walking, cycling, automobiles, buses, and rail to freight trucks, ships, aircraft, pipelines, and other specialized systems.

3
00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:20.638
No mode is best for every trip.

4
00:00:20.638 --> 00:00:28.309
Distance, volume, speed, cost, reliability, accessibility, safety, emissions, and the available network all shape suitability.

5
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:31.414
Multimodal and intermodal are related but distinct.

6
00:00:31.414 --> 00:00:34.763
A multimodal system offers more than one way to travel.

7
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:40.972
An intermodal trip connects modes through coordinated facilities, information, payment, or operations.

8
00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:44.321
A bicycle carried on a bus is an intermodal connection.

9
00:00:44.321 --> 00:00:49.374
Freight transferred in a standard container among ship, rail, and truck is another.

10
00:00:49.374 --> 00:00:50.957
The complete trip matters.

11
00:00:50.957 --> 00:00:56.253
A fast line-haul service is not accessible if a passenger cannot safely reach the stop.

12
00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:58.567
Transfers impose time and uncertainty.

13
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:03.924
Freight terminals create regional benefits and local truck, noise, and land-use effects.

14
00:01:03.924 --> 00:01:07.760
Planning therefore evaluates the network, not only the vehicle.

15
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.439
Mode choice also reflects public investment.

16
00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:18.231
Parking supply, street design, service frequency, pricing, and land-use location change the relative convenience of each option.

17
00:01:18.231 --> 00:01:23.467
Calling those results individual preference can hide the structure that produced them.

18
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:24.812
Torrance application.

19
00:01:24.812 --> 00:01:35.763
This local section is separate from the textbook adaptation and cites the Torrance Transit Tomorrow Plan and the City's Multi-Use Active Transportation Trail project page.

20
00:01:35.763 --> 00:01:42.038
The transit plan describes a bus network extending beyond Torrance into neighboring jurisdictions.

21
00:01:42.038 --> 00:01:50.364
The trail project describes a proposed active-transportation connection between the Regional Transit Center and Downtown Torrance.

22
00:01:50.364 --> 00:02:03.171
Together, those sources support an intermodal question: can a person walk, roll, or bicycle to the transit center, make a reliable regional connection, and complete the return trip at the needed hour?

23
00:02:03.171 --> 00:02:13.802
A review would examine crossings, wayfinding, bicycle parking or carriage, transfer time, lighting, disability access, fare information, and first-and-last-mile gaps.

24
00:02:13.802 --> 00:02:23.856
For freight, the same network perspective would identify truck routes, industrial access, curb conflicts, pavement needs, and exposure near homes or schools.

25
00:02:23.856 --> 00:02:28.979
Passenger and goods movement cannot be evaluated as if they use separate cities.

26
00:02:28.979 --> 00:02:34.615
This exercise does not claim that a proposed Torrance project resolves every connection.

27
00:02:34.615 --> 00:02:39.930
It demonstrates how chapter five's mode framework turns into a complete-trip audit.

28
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:51.265
This episode adapts chapter five of Transportation Policies, Programs and History by Audirac, Raley, Reiner, and Sharifi-Asl, published by Mavs Open Press under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.

29
00:02:51.265 --> 00:02:53.250
Third-party items are excluded.

30
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.196
Torrance sources are independently summarized.

31
00:02:56.196 --> 00:02:57.861
No endorsement is implied.
